---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About WhyTrashEarth and the purpose of WTE Wiki.">
  <section class="content">
     <h1>About WhyTrashEarth</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        WhyTrashEarth is an independent environmental initiative focused on solving
        real-world problems through design, strategy, documentation, engineering-minded
        thinking, and long-term commitment.
      </p>
    <section class="content-grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h2>What is WhyTrashEarth?</h2>
        <p>
          Founded in 2016, WhyTrashEarth exists to improve life on Earth by identifying
          overlooked problems, studying them deeply, and pursuing practical ways to make
          systems better.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rather than relying only on outrage, slogans, or surface-level activism,
          WhyTrashEarth is built around patience, precision, communication, and
          meaningful follow-through.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>Why this wiki exists</h2>
        <p>
          WTE Wiki serves as a growing public archive for the ideas, projects, campaigns,
          tools, concepts, and philosophy connected to WhyTrashEarth.
        </p>
        <p>
          Its purpose is to organize information clearly so visitors can better
          understand the scope of the work and how the different parts connect over time.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>Core mission</h2>
        <p>
          The mission of WhyTrashEarth is to improve environmental outcomes by addressing
          problems at their root whenever possible.
        </p>
        <p>
          This includes everything from air quality, waste, and infrastructure concerns
          to communication systems, public awareness, research organization, and tools
          that help people understand problems more clearly.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>How WhyTrashEarth approaches change</h2>
        <p>
          WhyTrashEarth emphasizes ethical, professional, and often direct approaches to
          change. That can include research, documentation, outreach, design, formal
          letters, collaboration, technical proposals, educational tools, and public-facing
          campaigns.
        </p>
        <p>
          The focus is not destruction. The focus is improvement.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>Guiding philosophy</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Build real solutions, not just reactions</li>
          <li>Respect people while still telling the truth</li>
          <li>Think long-term</li>
          <li>Document carefully</li>
          <li>Make complex ideas easier to understand</li>
          <li>Improve systems without losing humanity</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>Areas of work</h2>
        <p>WhyTrashEarth may document or engage with topics such as:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Air quality and vent cleanliness</li>
          <li>Waste reduction and environmental design</li>
          <li>Wildlife and ecosystem protection</li>
          <li>Infrastructure and overlooked maintenance issues</li>
          <li>Environmental communication and public education</li>
          <li>Archival, mapping, and knowledge organization</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>Not just one project</h2>
        <p>
          WhyTrashEarth is not limited to a single campaign. It is an evolving umbrella
          for many ideas, initiatives, experiments, and long-term efforts.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some projects are public, some are in development, and some may remain archival
          or conceptual until the right time.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>A living archive</h2>
        <p>
          This wiki is intended to grow over time. Some pages may begin as brief summaries
          and later expand into fuller project histories, research pages, timelines,
          concept notes, and related documentation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal is clarity, preservation, and a record of serious work in progress.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
